<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etowah River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Etowah River flows through north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and joins the Oostanaula at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coosa-river">Coosa River</a>. Its valley was home to the Etowah chiefdom, one of the great Mississippian centres, whose mounds still stand near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartersville-georgia">Cartersville</a>. In the 19th century it ran through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> lands before their removal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etowah-river.jpg" alt="Etowah River" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coosaheadwatersmap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
